"""Phase 2.1 NEXUS knowledge, bank A: what exists TODAY (status C). Every claim is checked against nexus_facts.py / PHASE_1_REPORT.md."""
from gen21_common import *

B = Bank("nexus_knowledge", "n21a")
d = B.d
K1 = "You are the NEXUS assistant. Describe NEXUS accurately: separate what is implemented today from what is only planned."
K2 = "You are an assistant that knows the NEXUS project. Be precise about what exists now and what is future work. Do not invent features."
K3 = "You are a helpful assistant with knowledge of the NEXUS platform. Say clearly when something is planned rather than built."

# ---- overview / stack ----
d("n21_overview", "I just joined the team. Can you give me the 30-second version of NEXUS?", "NEXUS is a full-stack personal intelligence and decision platform. It pulls projects, tasks, planning, notes, analytics, learning, career records and git activity into one app, then uses that data to surface risks, recommendations and what to work on next. The frontend is React with TypeScript, the backend is FastAPI on PostgreSQL, and the only AI model in it today is a small intent classifier.", sys=K1)
d("n21_overview", "is NEXUS a note-taking app or a project manager or what", "It's broader than either. Notes are one part (the knowledge area), and projects and tasks are another, but NEXUS also has a planner with focus sessions, analytics, risk detection, developer activity from local git repositories, learning and career tracking, and a Command Center that combines signals. Think of it as one place for personal planning and decision support.")
d("n21_overview", "Where does the 'intelligence' in 'personal intelligence platform' come from?", "Mostly from deterministic code over your own recorded data: analytics, a risk-detection engine that scores conditions with evidence, and recommendations built from them. The one machine-learning model is a DeBERTa intent classifier that routes a sentence to the right area. There is no generative language model in the application today.")
d("n21_overview", "How would you describe NEXUS to a non-technical friend?", "It's like a personal command post for your work and goals. You keep your projects, tasks, notes, learning goals and schedule in one app, and it points out what needs attention, such as a deadline with no time booked for it. You can also type or speak a request, and it works out which part of the app the request belongs to.")
d("n21_overview", "What problem is NEXUS trying to solve?", "Fragmentation. Tasks live in one tool, notes in another, analytics in a third, git history somewhere else. NEXUS keeps them together and connects them, so it can answer questions like what is at risk, what deserves focus next, and how your learning and career records relate to the work you do.")
d("n21_overview", "Is NEXUS finished?", "The README describes 14 completed development phases, from the foundation through ML, voice, the Command Center and hardening. Still, some README claims are broader than the code, and the generative assistant side is not built yet. So the core application is built, while the Qwen-based assistant is a plan.", sys=K3)
d("n21_stack", "Give me the tech stack for NEXUS in one list.", "- Frontend: React, TypeScript, Vite, Tailwind CSS, React Router, TanStack Query, Zustand, Recharts\n- Frontend tests: Vitest and React Testing Library\n- Backend: Python, FastAPI, SQLAlchemy, Pydantic, Alembic\n- Database: PostgreSQL (according to the backend configuration)\n- Auth: JWT access and refresh tokens\n- ML: microsoft/deberta-v3-base fine-tuned as an intent classifier, run on a pinned CPU build of PyTorch\n- Voice: the browser's Web Speech API and speechSynthesis")
d("n21_stack", "Why does the frontend use a relative /api/v1 URL?", "It keeps the browser same-origin. In development Vite proxies those calls to the FastAPI server, so there are no CORS or cookie headaches, and the frontend code doesn't need to know where the backend lives.")
d("n21_stack", "What's the layering in the NEXUS backend?", "The code is split into API routes, services, repositories, models and schemas. Routes are thin and sit under /api/v1, services hold the business logic, repositories handle database access, and Pydantic schemas validate the data going in and out.")
d("n21_stack", "How does NEXUS keep one user's data away from another's?", "Data is scoped to the signed-in user. Requests carry a bearer token, services work on owner-scoped records, and routes check role-based permissions. Security events go to an audit log, and in-memory rate limiting protects the API.")
d("n21_stack", "does nexus handle migrations", "Yes. The schema is managed with Alembic migrations (the audit counted 14 migration files), and the backend is configured for PostgreSQL.")
d("n21_stack", "I found a note saying NEXUS defaults to SQLite. Should I trust it?", "Not without checking. The backend code is configured for PostgreSQL through its postgres settings or a DATABASE_URL, and that is what the Alembic migrations target. A project note mentioned SQLite, so look at your own .env settings before assuming either one. How the dev database runs on a particular machine hasn't been verified.")
d("n21_stack", "Roughly how big is the test suite?", "The audit counted about 103 backend test files (pytest) and about 52 frontend test files (Vitest with React Testing Library). Those counts come from reading the repository; the suites were not run during the audit, so a pass rate is unknown.")
d("n21_stack", "Does NEXUS run offline?", "The design principle is local first, and the core features depend on your own backend and database rather than a cloud AI service. The one caveat is voice input: with Chrome or Edge the browser sends your audio to the vendor for transcription, so speech recognition is not offline.")

# ---- modules ----
d("n21_modules", "What can the planner do that the task list can't?", "The task list says what needs doing; the planner decides when. It handles weekly planning, your availability, time blocks, focus sessions and scheduling suggestions for tasks.")
d("n21_modules", "How does NEXUS know a deadline is at risk?", "A detection engine looks at your recorded data for conditions such as a deadline with no time booked for it, or planned work exceeding your available hours. Each finding is scored and keeps its evidence, and recommendations and attention signals are generated from the findings.")
d("n21_modules", "Does the developer module measure how many hours I coded?", "No, and that is deliberate. It scans local git repositories you register and analyses commits, branches, changed lines and languages, but it treats a commit as evidence of a commit rather than as hours worked.")
d("n21_modules", "Can NEXUS read any folder on my laptop for the developer module?", "Only repositories you register, and the backend runs local git against them. There is an optional path allowlist setting (DEVELOPER_PATH_ALLOWLIST) that restricts which paths may be registered. It is not a general file-reading feature.")
d("n21_modules", "Is there a place for storing notes and resources?", "Yes. The knowledge area holds notes, concepts and resources, and global search can find them along with projects, tasks and other records.")
d("n21_modules", "What does the career module record?", "A career profile, career records and portfolio evidence. Together with the learning module (goals, skills, activities and skill gaps) it lets you link what you learn to where you want to go.")
d("n21_modules", "What exactly does global search cover?", "Projects, tasks, notes, resources, concepts, repositories, learning goals, skills, calendar items, risks and recommendations, and the results are limited to your own data.")
d("n21_modules", "How do I open the command palette?", "Press Ctrl+K, or Cmd+K on macOS. It gives keyboard access to navigation, quick actions and record search.")
d("n21_modules", "What's on the Command Center page?", "A priority queue, measured panels, recommendations, recent activity, quick actions and an ML insight panel. Its purpose is to answer what needs attention and what to work on next. It is mostly read-oriented in the code.")
d("n21_modules", "Does the Command Center browse the internet for me?", "No. The README mentions web intelligence for that phase, but the audit found no outbound web client or search route in the backend, so the Command Center works from your own data and the classifier's insight panel.")
d("n21_modules", "which pages exist in the app", "The audit lists feature areas rather than a full page inventory: projects, tasks, planner, knowledge, analytics, risks and recommendations, developer, learning, career, search, a command palette, quick actions, the Command Center and the /assistant page. For the exact route list, check the frontend router.")
d("n21_modules", "What do focus sessions do?", "They are part of the planner: you block out time to work on something, and the system can record the work session. They tie tasks to real time rather than to a due date alone.")

# ---- classifier ----
d("n21_ml", "What is the NEXUS classifier, in plain words?", "A small model that reads one sentence and says which part of NEXUS the request is about, together with a confidence. If someone types 'remind me to pay rent', it should say that this is task-related. It does not write anything back.")
d("n21_ml", "Which base model is the classifier built on?", "microsoft/deberta-v3-base, fine-tuned during Phase 10 as a 14-class intent classifier.")
d("n21_ml", "Could the DeBERTa model write me a summary of my week?", "No. It is a classifier: its output is a label and a confidence over 14 intents, not text. Writing a summary would need a generative model, and NEXUS doesn't have one yet.")
d("n21_ml", "how many intents does it know", "Fourteen, in a taxonomy called nexo_intents.v1: task_manage, project_manage, schedule_plan, knowledge_capture, knowledge_lookup, analytics_insight, risk_query, developer_intel, learning_track, career_track, account_admin, code_assist, deep_reasoning and out_of_scope.")
d("n21_ml", "Which intents don't lead anywhere today?", "code_assist and deep_reasoning. The classifier can recognise them, but twelve of the fourteen intents map to an existing NEXUS service and these two have no destination, so the system answers generation_unavailable.")
d("n21_ml", "Is 97.4% accuracy good enough to trust it?", "Be careful with that number. It was measured on a held-out test set that was generated from templates. On 56 hand-written natural-language requests it got 42 right, which is 75%. So real accuracy is closer to the second number, and the gap shows how much synthetic data flatters a model.")
d("n21_ml", "What went wrong in the 14 misses on the hand-written set?", "The audit notes that several misses collapsed into risk_query, and some wrong predictions came with confidence above the 0.90 threshold. I don't have a per-request breakdown beyond that.")
d("n21_ml", "Does the 0.90 threshold make the classifier safe?", "It helps but doesn't make it safe. Below 0.90 the request is reported as uncertain instead of being routed. However, some wrong predictions scored above 0.90, and the threshold was picked on synthetic data, so it is a routing guard and not a calibrated probability.")
d("n21_ml", "What happens if I type something the classifier doesn't understand?", "If confidence falls below 0.90 you get an 'uncertain' response with runner-up intents instead of a route. If the sentence is unrelated to the app, the intended label is out_of_scope.")
d("n21_ml", "Does the classifier remember what I asked earlier?", "No. Only the current utterance is sent, and the model maps a single string to a single label. The 20-turn history is kept in the browser purely for display.")
d("n21_ml", "Is the classifier loaded every time I make a request?", "No. The checkpoint is loaded once per backend process, lazily, through a runtime singleton. Settings like ML_ENABLED, ML_MODEL_PATH and ML_DEVICE control it.")
d("n21_ml", "What if the ML checkpoint is missing on my machine?", "The ML endpoints answer 503 ml_unavailable and the rest of the app keeps working. torch is imported lazily, so the backend still boots and serves all non-ML routes.")
d("n21_ml", "Which endpoints expose the classifier?", "GET /api/v1/ml/status tells you whether the classifier is loaded, and POST /api/v1/ml/route classifies one text and returns a routing decision. They need a bearer token, and the classifier routes need the analytics.read permission.")
d("n21_ml", "Does the classifier need a GPU?", "No. The backend uses a pinned CPU build of PyTorch, and the ML device setting defaults to auto, using CUDA only if a working build exists. The classifier is small enough that NEXUS doesn't require a GPU.")
d("n21_ml", "Where does the training code for DeBERTa live?", "In backend/ml: dataset schema and taxonomy, training configuration, checkpoint and manifest handling, evaluation, train.py and a small-model config. Datasets and artifacts are gitignored.")
d("n21_ml", "Why did the model do so much worse on real phrasing?", "The training and test data were generated from templates, so the model learned those shapes very well. Real people phrase things differently, which exposed the gap between 0.974 and 75%. The lesson recorded in the audit is that template-generated data is a risk, and the same risk applies to any new training set.")

# ---- actions ----
d("n21_actions", "If I say 'add a task to review the budget', what does NEXUS do?", "The sentence can go through the propose step, which classifies it and, using hand-written slot extraction, builds a proposal such as creating a task with a title. You see the proposal and decide. Nothing is created until you confirm it through the confirm step.")
d("n21_actions", "Can NEXUS take actions without asking me?", "No. The only path that executes a sentence is propose then confirm, and confirm needs you to approve the specific proposal. There is no free-form execution.")
d("n21_actions", "What are the endpoints for actions?", "POST /api/v1/ml/action/propose builds a proposal and POST /api/v1/ml/action/confirm executes it, both authenticated.")
d("n21_actions", "Who decides what an action may do?", "A closed table of action kinds covers things like tasks, projects, knowledge, learning, planner, developer repositories and account writes. Each kind has a destructive flag, and confirm calls the owner-scoped service for that kind. The model never composes arbitrary calls.")
d("n21_actions", "Is a language model extracting the task title from my sentence?", "No. Slot extraction is done by hand-written parsers in the app's ML actions package. The code's own notes say that turning an utterance into an exact service call is slot filling that normally needs a second model or per-utterance parsers, and NEXUS chose parsers.")
d("n21_actions", "What does the destructive flag do?", "It marks action kinds that remove or overwrite data so the proposal can be presented as destructive. It is part of why nothing runs without an explicit confirmation.")
d("n21_actions", "Can I delete everything by voice?", "A voice request is only classified. Any delete-type action would have to appear as a proposal, be flagged as destructive, and wait for you to confirm it yourself.")
d("n21_actions", "What does the interface say when I ask for something NEXUS can't do yet?", "For coding help or deep reasoning, the response is generation_unavailable, which the UI presents as a stated capability gap rather than an error.")
d("n21_actions", "why is propose and confirm separate from routing", "Routing only says where a request belongs; it changes nothing. Proposing prepares a concrete change that you can inspect, and confirming performs it. Splitting them follows the design principle that AI actions that change data need user confirmation.")
d("n21_actions", "Does confirming an action go through special AI code?", "No. Confirm calls the matching owner-scoped service, the same kind of service the normal REST routes use. The only AI involved earlier is the classifier that picked the intent.")

# ---- voice ----
d("n21_voice", "How does NEXUS hear me?", "Through the browser's own SpeechRecognition (webkit-prefixed in Safari). NEXUS doesn't run a speech model itself.")
d("n21_voice", "Does NEXUS use Whisper for speech?", "No. Speech recognition is the browser's Web Speech API, and the project's one-model rule kept Whisper out. In Chrome and Edge the audio is transcribed on the vendor's servers.")
d("n21_voice", "Is voice private?", "Partly. Transcription in Chrome and Edge happens on Google's or Microsoft's servers, so your audio leaves the device. The NEXUS backend only receives the resulting text. Speech output is local, using the operating system voice.")
d("n21_voice", "I'm using Firefox and the mic button doesn't work. Is that a bug?", "Probably not a bug: Firefox has no SpeechRecognition support, so voice input isn't available there. You can still type your request on the /assistant page.")
d("n21_voice", "What does the assistant say back when I use voice?", "Today it reads a routing statement, like 'Routing to Task', using speechSynthesis. It isn't a composed answer, because no generative model sits behind it.")
d("n21_voice", "How many turns does the assistant page keep?", "Up to 20 in the browser, in a bounded Zustand store that is never persisted. Refresh the page and the log is gone, and the backend never receives the history.")
d("n21_voice", "What are the states of the voice assistant?", "Idle, listening, processing, speaking and error, managed by a state machine in the assistant feature's hook. The page also shows a latest-decision card and a conversation log.")
d("n21_voice", "Can I ask the voice assistant to explain a concept?", "Not today. It routes your sentence to a NEXUS area; it can't explain, reason or hold a conversation, because there is no generative model behind it.")
d("n21_voice", "Is there voice code on the backend?", "No. The browser transcribes and speaks, and the backend sees only text at POST /api/v1/ml/route.")
d("n21_voice", "What happens if my browser has no speech support?", "The page falls back to typing. Text input is always available, which matters in browsers such as Firefox.")

# ---- absent today ----
d("n21_absent", "Does NEXUS have a chat window?", "Not yet. The /assistant page shows routing decisions and is written to say that NEXUS routes rather than answers. A chat view is a planned addition.")
d("n21_absent", "Can I talk to an AI inside NEXUS about my notes?", "Not today. There is no generative model, no chat endpoint and no conversation backend. You can search your notes and use the classifier to route a request, but you can't discuss them with an assistant yet.")
d("n21_absent", "The README mentions Ollama. Where is it configured?", "It isn't. A search of the backend application found no Ollama code, and the requirements file says Ollama is deliberately absent. The README overstates it, so Ollama should be treated as not integrated.")
d("n21_absent", "Does NEXUS have tools like file access or a terminal?", "No. There is no tool registry and no filesystem, terminal or browser tools. What exists is its own set of REST services, the propose and confirm actions, and a developer module that runs local git.")
d("n21_absent", "Is there a web search feature in NEXUS?", "Not as far as the code shows. The README mentions web intelligence, but the audit found no outbound HTTP client or web-search route in the backend, only URL parsing helpers. The global search in NEXUS covers your own data only.")
d("n21_absent", "Can NEXUS send an email for me?", "No. It has no email capability and no tool for sending messages. Confirmed actions are limited to creating and changing its own records.")
d("n21_absent", "Where's the LLM setting in the admin panel?", "There isn't one, because NEXUS has no language model today. The settings that exist for ML concern the DeBERTa classifier: ML_ENABLED, ML_MODEL_PATH, ML_DEVICE and the confidence threshold.")
d("n21_absent", "Does NEXUS store my conversations on the server?", "No. The assistant history lives only in the browser, is capped at 20 turns and is not persisted. There is no server-side conversation store at present.")
d("n21_absent", "can nexus code for me", "Not yet. The classifier can recognise a code_assist request, but NEXUS has nowhere to send it, so the answer is generation_unavailable. A fine-tuned Qwen model is planned to fill that role.")
d("n21_absent", "Is there an API key I need for NEXUS's AI?", "No. NEXUS uses no cloud AI service. The classifier runs inside the backend process from a local checkpoint, and the project's rules excluded cloud LLM SDKs.")
d("n21_absent", "How does NEXUS summarise articles?", "It doesn't. Summarising needs a generative model, and the application has none. There is also no web tool for fetching articles.")
d("n21_absent", "Does the Command Center have an AI that explains its priorities?", "The priorities come from deterministic logic over measured data, plus an ML insight panel built on the classifier. No language model writes explanations today.")

# ---- design principles / history ----
d("n21_principles", "What does 'AI with a purpose' mean in the NEXUS design?", "It means using AI only where it adds value and keeping the core deterministic. For instance, risk detection is rule-based with evidence, and the one model is a narrow classifier for routing.")
d("n21_principles", "What does 'evidence over assumptions' mean?", "The system distinguishes what it knows from what it estimates. Risk findings keep their evidence, and the developer module treats commits as evidence of commits and not as hours worked.")
d("n21_principles", "Why did the project refuse a second model for so long?", "The one-model rule kept behaviour local, explainable and tied to one measurable artefact. Each extra model adds weights to train, serve and trust, which is also why voice uses the browser instead of Whisper.")
d("n21_principles", "What are the six design principles?", "Local first, AI with a purpose, modular by design, evidence over assumptions, user control, and security as a foundation.")
d("n21_principles", "How does user control show up in the product?", "Mainly through propose and confirm: AI-derived actions that change data are shown as proposals and only run after you confirm. Destructive ones are flagged.")
d("n21_principles", "What does 'modular by design' look like in the code?", "Routes, services, repositories, models and schemas are separated, and each feature area (tasks, planner, knowledge, analytics, risks, developer, learning, career, search, ML) has its own router and service layer.")
d("n21_history", "Which phase added the DeBERTa classifier?", "Phase 10, ML training. Phase 11 integrated it into the application, and Phase 12 added the browser-based voice work.")
d("n21_history", "What did Phase 12 actually deliver?", "Browser voice: Web Speech API for input, speechSynthesis for output and the /assistant page. The README labels the phase 'Voice + Ollama', but no Ollama code was found, so only the voice part is verified.")
d("n21_history", "Did Phase 13 add web intelligence?", "The README title says 'Command Center + Web Intelligence', but the audit found only the Command Center side in the code. No outbound web client or search route was found in the backend.")
d("n21_history", "Why should I double-check README claims about the AI features?", "Because the audit found two that the code doesn't back up: Ollama integration and web intelligence. The README is useful for intent, but for what exists you should check the code and docs.")
d("n21_history", "Are there known schema mismatches?", "The audit mentions drift between the frontend and backend MlStatusRead schema, documented in the Phase 12 voice document. It was reported, not fixed.")

if __name__ == "__main__":
    save("nexus21_a_test", B.records())
